          <text><![CDATA[<p>Worked Solution version 1:</p>
<p>In this case, we have three different options for the team:</p>
<p>1. The team has no men at all and is made up of only women. We need to choose {@numtot@} women from a total of {@women@} to choose from. This can be done in \(^{{@women@}}C_{{@numtot@}} = {@binomial(women,numtot)@}\) ways.</p>
<p>2. The team has exactly one man. This is the same as part 1 and the number of possibilities is {@ta1@}.</p>
<p>3. The team has exactly two men. This is the same as part 2 and the number of possibilities is {@ta2@}.</p>
<p>4. The team has exactly three men. We calculate the number of possibilities in the same way as before:</p>
<p>We need exactly three men in a team of {@numtot@} people. We have to choose 3 men from a possible {@men@} men in the working group and the remaining {@numtot-2@} members of the presentation team have to be women from a possible total of {@women@} women in the working group.</p>
<p>The number of possibilities for the men is \(^{{@men@}}C_{3} = {@binomial(men,3)@}\).</p>
<p>The number of possibilities for the women is \(^{{@women@}}C_{{@numtot-3@}} = {@binomial(women,numtot-3)@}\).</p>
<p>Therefore, the total number of different teams is \(^{{@men@}}C_{3} \cdot ^{{@women@}}C_{{@numtot-3@}} = {@binomial(men,3)@} \cdot {@binomial(women,numtot-3)@} = {@binomial(men,3)*binomial(women,numtot-3)@}\).</p>
<p>As the four options don't overlap, we now add the three results and get a total of \({@binomial(women,numtot)@} + {@ta1@}+{@ta2@}+{@binomial(men,3)*binomial(women,numtot-3)@} = {@ta3@}\).</p>]]></text>
