          <text><![CDATA[<p>Worked Solution:</p>
<p>We need at least two women in the presentation team and we have {@women@} to choose from. The number of ways in which we can do so is \(^{{@women@}}C_{2} = {@binomial(women,2)@}\).</p>
<p>Similarly, we need at least two men in the presentation team and we have {@men@} to choose from. The number of ways in which we can do so is \(^{{@men@}}C_{2} = {@binomial(men,2)@}\).</p>
<p>The gender of the remaining {@numtot-4@} members of the presentation team is indifferent and we have {@women - 2@} women and {@men - 2@} men to choose from. So, we have to choose {@numtot-4@} from a possible {@total-4@} people, which can be done in \(^{{@total-4@}}C_{{@numtot-4@}} = {@binomial(total-4,numtot-4)@}\) ways.</p>
<p>Therefore, the total number of ways in which the presentation team can be formed is \(^{{@women@}}C_{2} \cdot ^{{@men@}}C_{2} \cdot ^{{@total-4@}}C_{{@numtot-4@}} = {@binomial(women,2)@} \cdot {@binomial(men,2)@} \cdot {@binomial(total-4,numtot-4)@} = {@binomial(women,2)*binomial(men,2)*binomial(total-4,numtot-4)@}\).</p>]]></text>
